/*****************************************************************************
    inlineSelfT -- AN INLINED CALLEE'S SELF-CALL RE-ENTERS THE WHOLE ENCLOSING
    FUNCTION, REPLAYING THE DRIVER'S PREAMBLE ON EVERY RECURSION. 2026-08-05.

    ⚠ PRE-EXISTING, NOT INTRODUCED BY THE FRAME BRACKET. Verified the expensive
    but honest way: the offending source files were reverted to b7a01c1, the
    tree REBUILT, and the crashing variant still exited 139. This is not a
    regression from today's work.

    ⚠ CONFIRMED BY IR, NOT INFERRED FROM A SYMPTOM -- which matters here because
    TWO attempts to confirm it behaviourally were confounded and one of them
    LOOKED like a refutation. `INCANT_JIT_DUMP=2` on the safe variant:

        define i32 @jitFn0() {
        entry:
          store i32 0, ptr <rbOut>          <- THE DRIVER'S OWN FIRST STATEMENT
          store i32 42, ptr <rbLoc>         <- the INLINED callee's body starts
          ...
        then:
          %bindArg   = call <jitBindArgRT>(...)
          call <jitSaveFrameRT>(<callee>)
          %selfcall  = call i32 @jitFn0()   <- RE-ENTERS AT `entry`
          call <jitRestoreFrameRT>(<callee>)

    @jitFn0 is the function built for the DRIVER -- it is the only function in
    the module -- so `call @jitFn0()` replays every statement the driver ran
    before the call. Emit-on-walk inlines an ordinary callee into the caller's
    builder, so a self-call inside that inlined body has no separate function to
    target; jitEmitSelfCall emits CreateCall(gJitCurrentFn) and gJitCurrentFn is
    the DRIVER's.

    THE DISCRIMINATING EXPERIMENT, one variable moved:
        guard reset INSIDE the driver   -> the reset replays forever  -> 139
        guard reset OUTSIDE the driver  -> nothing harmful replays    -> exit 0
    Only the location of one statement changed. That pair is the proof, and the
    safe half is what this fixture runs.

    ⚠⚠ AND THE PART THAT MAKES THIS URGENT RATHER THAN TIDY: displayForm IS
    CORRECT BY ACCIDENT OF TOPOLOGY. dfDrive's body is exactly one statement --
    the call itself -- so re-entering the whole function happens to be
    equivalent to re-entering the callee's body. Rung JC is green for a reason
    that is true of its driver rather than of the mechanism. THE SAME CLASS AS
    `continue` APPEARING TO WORK because both arms fell through to the back
    edge, and it will bite the moment a driver has a preamble -- which is the
    NORMAL case, and precisely the shape genParse's emitted rules will have.

    ⚠ THE CRASHING VARIANT IS NOT RUN HERE, BY RULE H5: a fixture that dies
    takes every check below it with it. To reproduce it by hand, move
    `rbDepth = 1;` from the top level INTO rbDrive as its first statement and
    run with a bound. Expect 139, no degrade message, and no output after
    `jitRunAction: entering`.

    NOT IN SCOPE, observed and NOT chased (recorded so it is not lost): a
    `print` placed in the driver ABOVE the inlined call produced NO output in
    the jitted half while the oracle printed it once. That is its own question.

    SENTINEL AT THE FOOT (standing harness rule H2).
*****************************************************************************/
Start();
include(unitTests);
include(generate);
include(utilities);

search reset stack Grokking;
search Generating UnitTests bcOPs Utilities list;

register(InlineSelf);
define
    rbDepth;
    rbArg;
    rbOut;
    rb argument code={
        rbLoc = 42;
        if rbDepth > 0;
            rbDepth = rbDepth - 1;
            rb(argument);
        rbOut = rbLoc;
        };
    /*  THE PREAMBLE IS DELIBERATELY HARMLESS. `rbOut = 0;` replays on every
        recursion and costs nothing; the crashing variant differs only in that
        its replayed statement is the loop guard's reset.  */
    rbDrive code={
        rbOut = 0;
        rbOut = rb(rbArg);
        };
    ;

rbArg  = 7;
rbDepth = 1;

print "== jitted: inlined callee recursing, HARMLESS driver preamble ==":;
testing(rbDrive);

print "inlineSelfT SENTINEL -- run reached the end":;
stop();
